/**
 * @defgroup MCC_Specification Music-Domain Specification
 * @brief Invariants shared by every MCC music-theory module.
 *
 * Status: approved (Phase 2). Every rule below is normative; later phases
 * implement and test against it. Identifiers such as
 * `SPEC-OCT-1` are referenced from unit tests.
 *
 * Terminology revision (after Phase 4): the spelled letter + accidental type
 * is `NoteName` and `PitchClass` is the integer class 0-11, following the
 * standard music-theory meaning of "pitch class". The rules are unchanged.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecTerms Terminology
 *
 * MCC builds absolute pitch in two steps:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * Letter + Accidental   -> NoteName   (written spelling, no octave)
 * NoteName + Octave     -> Pitch      (written, absolute)
 * @endcode
 *
 * | Type | Meaning | Example |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `MCC::Letter` | Diatonic letter name | `D` |
 * | `MCC::Accidental` | Chromatic alteration of a letter | sharp (+1) |
 * | `MCC::NoteName` | Letter + accidental, spelling preserved | `D#` |
 * | `MCC::Pitch` | Note name + octave | `D#4` |
 * | `MCC::PitchClass` | Pitch class 0-11, spelling discarded | `3` |
 * | `MCC::ChromaticIndex` | Absolute semitone position of a pitch | `63` |
 * | `MCC::Interval` | Directed diatonic + chromatic distance | ascending major third |
 * | `MCC::NoteValue` | Exact written length: power-of-two base plus dots | dotted quarter = `3/8` of a whole note |
 * | `MCC::Note` | Written pitch + written note value | `C#4`, dotted eighth |
 * | `MCC::Meter` | Written time signature and beat interpretation | compound `6/8` |
 *
 * `MCC::Note` is not an alternate absolute-pitch type: MCC::Pitch represents
 * height and spelling; MCC::Note adds a rhythmic value.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecOctave Octave convention
 *
 * - SPEC-OCT-1: MCC uses scientific pitch notation. Middle C is `C4`.
 * - SPEC-OCT-2: Under the standard tuning `A4` is 440 Hz.
 * - SPEC-OCT-3: The octave number belongs to the letter, not to the sounding
 *   pitch. Octaves change between `B` and `C`. `B#3` is written in octave 3
 *   and sounds as `C4`; `Cb4` is written in octave 4 and sounds as `B3`.
 * - SPEC-OCT-4: Octaves are signed 8-bit values in `[-128, 127]`. Operations
 *   that would leave this range produce an invalid result (see errors).
 *
 * @section MCCSpecEquality Written equality versus enharmonic equivalence
 *
 * - SPEC-EQ-1: `operator==` compares written spelling: letter, accidental
 *   and, for pitches, octave. `C# != Db` and `C#4 != Db4`.
 * - SPEC-EQ-2: Enharmonic equivalence is always explicit:
 *   `MCC::IsEnharmonic(a, b)`. For note names it compares pitch
 *   classes; for pitches it compares chromatic indices. `B#3` and `C4` are
 *   enharmonic; `B#3` and `C3` are not.
 * - SPEC-EQ-3: No implicit conversion exists between `NoteName`,
 *   `Pitch`, `PitchClass` and `ChromaticIndex`.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecOrdering Ordering
 *
 * - SPEC-ORD-1: Letters are ordered `C D E F G A B` with diatonic indices
 *   0-6 and natural semitone offsets `0 2 4 5 7 9 11`.
 * - SPEC-ORD-2: Diatonic index of a pitch = `octave * 7 + letterIndex`.
 * - SPEC-ORD-3: Chromatic index of a pitch =
 *   `(octave + 1) * 12 + letterSemitone + accidental`.
 * - SPEC-ORD-4: Pitch class of a note name =
 *   `(letterSemitone + accidental)`
 *   reduced modulo 12 into `[0, 11]`.
 * - SPEC-ORD-5: `operator<` is written order, used for sorting and
 *   containers: octave, then letter, then accidental. It is not pitch
 *   height: `B#3 < Cb4` in written order although `B#3` sounds higher.
 * - SPEC-ORD-6: Pitch height is compared through chromatic indices, with
 *   ties broken by written order to keep sorting deterministic.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecAccidentals Accidental range
 *
 * - SPEC-ACC-1: Accidentals range from -4 (quadruple flat) to +4 (quadruple
 *   sharp), matching the historical MCC token model (9 values per letter).
 * - SPEC-ACC-2: Natural (0) is a distinct written accidental; the
 *   formatting layer decides whether it is printed.
 * - SPEC-ACC-3: Any operation whose result needs an accidental outside
 *   `[-4, +4]` produces an invalid result; it never respells silently.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecChromatic Absolute chromatic coordinate
 *
 * - SPEC-CHR-1: `MCC::ChromaticIndex` is a signed 16-bit semitone count with
 *   origin `C-1 = 0`, so `C0 = 12`, `C4 = 60` and `A4 = 69`. The numbering
 *   coincides with MIDI note numbers so values are familiar when debugging.
 * - SPEC-CHR-2: The full octave and accidental ranges fit in the type
 *   (`-127 * 12 - 4 = -1528` to `128 * 12 + 11 + 4 = 1551`).
 * - SPEC-CHR-3: A chromatic index is not restricted to the MIDI range and
 *   MCC exposes no MIDI types. MIDILAR converts a pitch to a MIDI note
 *   number by taking its chromatic index and checking that it lies in
 *   `[0, 127]`.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecIntervals Intervals
 *
 * - SPEC-INT-1: An interval stores a diatonic step count and a chromatic
 *   semitone count; quality and number are derived from both.
 * - SPEC-INT-2: Intervals are directed: ascending, descending or unison.
 *   Number is 1-based (`1` = unison, `8` = octave) and always positive; the
 *   direction is stored separately.
 * - SPEC-INT-3: Simple intervals have numbers 1-8; compound intervals have
 *   numbers above 8 and reduce to simple ones by removing octaves
 *   (`10 -> 3`).
 * - SPEC-INT-4: Qualities are perfect, major, minor, augmented and
 *   diminished, with augmented and diminished repeatable up to four times.
 *   Perfect applies to unisons, fourths, fifths and octaves (and compounds);
 *   major/minor to the rest.
 * - SPEC-INT-5: A diminished unison is not produced: the interval from `C`
 *   to `Cb` is a descending augmented unison.
 * - SPEC-INT-6: Inversion of a simple interval satisfies
 *   `number + inverted number = 9` and swaps major/minor and
 *   augmented/diminished. Because of SPEC-INT-5, an augmented octave
 *   inverts to an augmented unison in the opposite direction.
 * - SPEC-INT-7: Transposition preserves spelling: `pitch + interval` moves
 *   the letter by the diatonic count and sets the accidental required by the
 *   chromatic count.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecErrors Error handling without exceptions
 *
 * MCC never throws and never has undefined behavior on invalid input.
 *
 * - SPEC-ERR-1: `Accidental`, `NoteName`, `Pitch`, `Interval`, `NoteValue`,
 *   `Note` and `Meter` each have one canonical invalid value and an
 *   `IsValid()` query. `Letter` is a closed enumeration and is always valid.
 * - SPEC-ERR-2: Default construction produces the invalid value.
 * - SPEC-ERR-3: Construction from checked inputs (integers, text) that are
 *   out of range produces the invalid value.
 * - SPEC-ERR-4: Invalid values propagate: any operation with an invalid
 *   operand returns an invalid result.
 * - SPEC-ERR-5: Operations whose result is unrepresentable (octave or
 *   accidental overflow) return an invalid result.
 * - SPEC-ERR-6: All invalid values of a type compare equal to each other and
 *   unequal to every valid value. `IsEnharmonic()` with an invalid operand is
 *   false.
 * - SPEC-ERR-7: Numeric queries on an invalid value (for example
 *   `ChromaticIndex()`) return a documented sentinel and must not be relied
 *   on; debug builds may assert.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecEmbedded Embedded memory and object-size constraints
 *
 * - SPEC-EMB-1: Fundamental value types are trivially copyable, have no
 *   virtual functions and never allocate.
 * - SPEC-EMB-2: Size budgets: `Letter`, `Accidental` and `PitchClass`
 *   1 byte, `NoteName` at most 2 bytes, `Pitch` at most 4 bytes,
 *   `Interval` at most 4 bytes; scale and chord patterns 8 bytes, `Scale`
 *   and `Chord` at most 12 bytes, `KeySignature` 1 byte, `Key` at most
 *   4 bytes, `NoteValue` at most 2 bytes, `Note` at most 6 bytes and `Meter`
 *   at most 3 bytes. Enforced with `static_assert` in the unit tests and in
 *   the library build for every target, AVR and Arm included (`src/MCC.cpp`).
 * - SPEC-EMB-3: Construction, comparison, transposition and interval
 *   arithmetic are `constexpr`.
 * - SPEC-EMB-4: Core code uses no RTTI, exceptions, iostreams or
 *   floating point; only tuning/frequency APIs use `float`.
 * - SPEC-EMB-5: Catalog data is `constexpr` static data placed in program
 *   memory on AVR through `FOUNDATION_FLASH` and read only through
 *   `Foundation::Utils::Flash`, so it occupies no RAM.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecScales Scales
 *
 * - SPEC-SCL-1: A scale pattern has 1 to 12 degrees with strictly ascending
 *   semitones inside one octave; the first degree is the perfect unison.
 * - SPEC-SCL-2: Each degree stores diatonic steps as well as semitones, so a
 *   scale spelled from any root keeps one letter per step: `E` major contains
 *   `G#`, never `Ab`. Repeated steps (`b5 5` in the blues scale) are allowed.
 * - SPEC-SCL-3: A degree whose spelling needs an accidental outside
 *   `[-4, +4]` is the invalid note name; the scale itself stays valid.
 * - SPEC-SCL-4: Catalog identifiers are stable and never reused. Names and
 *   aliases are unique and matched exactly by `Scales::Find()`.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecChords Chords
 *
 * - SPEC-CHD-1: A chord pattern has 1 to 7 tones with strictly ascending
 *   semitones below two octaves; the first tone is the perfect unison.
 * - SPEC-CHD-2: Each tone keeps its written number, so extensions are spelled
 *   as compound intervals: the ninth of `C9` is `D`, written as a ninth.
 * - SPEC-CHD-3: Inversion `k` places tone `k + 1` in the bass and raises each
 *   earlier tone by whole octaves until the voicing ascends (close position).
 * - SPEC-CHD-4: Catalog identifiers are stable and never reused. Names,
 *   symbols and aliases are unique; `Chords::Find()` matches names and aliases
 *   exactly.
 * - SPEC-CHD-5: `Chords::FromScale()` keeps a chord only when every tone is
 *   written in the scale; membership is by spelling, never enharmonic.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecKeys Keys
 *
 * - SPEC-KEY-1: A key signature has zero to seven sharps (F C G D A E B) or
 *   flats (B E A D G C F); anything else is the invalid signature.
 * - SPEC-KEY-2: A key is a tonic and one of the seven diatonic modes, modelled
 *   apart from its scale. Its signature follows from the circle of fifths; a
 *   tonic that would need more than seven sharps or flats is invalid.
 * - SPEC-KEY-3: Spelling in a key uses the key's letters for diatonic pitch
 *   classes. A chromatic pitch class alters a diatonic note by one semitone,
 *   choosing the fewest accidentals and, on a tie, a raised note in sharp
 *   keys (and C) or a lowered note in flat keys.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecRhythm Written rhythm
 *
 * - SPEC-RHY-1: A `NoteValue` base is a power-of-two fraction of a whole
 *   note, from whole (`1/1`) through 256th (`1/256`). Other base
 *   denominators are invalid.
 * - SPEC-RHY-2: A note value has zero to four augmentation dots. Every dot
 *   adds half of the preceding addition, so `base` with `d` dots is exactly
 *   `(2^(d+1) - 1) / (baseDenominator * 2^d)` of a whole note. The result is
 *   represented as an exact reduced integer fraction, never as floating
 *   point.
 * - SPEC-RHY-3: Written identity includes both the base and the dot count;
 *   ordering compares the exact duration. Invalid values sort after valid
 *   values, following the other MCC value types.
 * - SPEC-RHY-4: `NoteValue` describes notation only. Tempo, wall-clock
 *   duration, scheduling, MIDI clock and PPQN conversion belong outside the
 *   type and, where protocol or execution is involved, outside MCC.
 * - SPEC-RHY-5: `Note` combines exactly one valid written `Pitch` and one
 *   valid `NoteValue`. Invalid input produces the canonical invalid note;
 *   constructors that omit the value use a quarter note.
 * - SPEC-RHY-6: Note construction from letter, accidental, note name or pitch
 *   preserves spelling. Copy-style pitch movement preserves the note value,
 *   and changing the note value preserves the pitch.
 * - SPEC-RHY-7: MCC never consumes a MIDI-note type, conditionally or
 *   otherwise. A MIDI number has no unique spelling. MIDILAR owns conversion
 *   to `MCC::Note` and must receive a key or another explicit spelling policy,
 *   plus the desired `NoteValue`.
 * - SPEC-RHY-8: A `Meter` preserves a numerator in `[1, 255]` and an undotted
 *   power-of-two denominator from whole through 256th. Written equality does
 *   not reduce the signature: `3/4 != 6/8`; equal measure length is an
 *   explicit `HasSameMeasureDuration()` query.
 * - SPEC-RHY-9: Numerators one through four are simple. Numerators at least
 *   six and divisible by three are compound, with three denominator pulses
 *   per dotted beat. Other numerators are irregular: their grouping is not
 *   inferred and must later be supplied by a rhythm pattern.
 * - SPEC-RHY-10: A meter exposes its exact reduced measure length as a
 *   fraction of a whole note. It contains no tempo, clock, PPQN or playback
 *   state. A compound signature with denominator `1` is unsupported because
 *   its beat would exceed the largest current `NoteValue`.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecText Text formatting and buffer policy
 *
 * - SPEC-TXT-1: The core API never requires `std::string`. Formatting writes
 *   into a caller-provided `char` buffer.
 * - SPEC-TXT-2: Formatting follows `snprintf` semantics: it returns the
 *   length the full text needs, writes at most `size - 1` characters and
 *   always null-terminates when `size > 0`.
 * - SPEC-TXT-3: Default output is ASCII: `#` sharp, `b` flat, repeated for
 *   multiples (`C##4`, `Ebbb3`); natural is omitted unless requested. Unicode
 *   symbols are opt-in through `NotationOptions`.
 * - SPEC-TXT-4: Parsing accepts every output form in every supported encoding
 *   and a few common variants: a letter in either case, `x` for the double
 *   sharp, `n`/`♮` for the natural and a leading `+` on intervals. Sharps and
 *   flats never mix; malformed text, including malformed UTF-8 or UTF-16,
 *   returns the invalid value.
 * - SPEC-TXT-5: The output encoding follows the buffer's character type:
 *   `char` holds ASCII or UTF-8, `char16_t` UTF-16 and `char32_t` UTF-32.
 *   Lengths and capacities count code units, and truncation never splits a
 *   multi-unit character.
 *
 * @section MCCSpecTests Invariant-to-test map
 *
 * | Invariants | Planned test suite | Phase |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | SPEC-ORD-1, ORD-4, ACC-1..3 | `NoteNameTests` (exhaustive 7 x 9 spellings) | 3 |
 * | SPEC-EQ-1..3, ORD-5..6 | `PitchComparisonTests` | 3-4 |
 * | SPEC-OCT-1..4, ORD-2..3, CHR-1..2 | `PitchTests` (octave and B#/Cb boundaries) | 4 |
 * | SPEC-OCT-2 | `TuningTests` (A4 = 440 Hz, C4 ~ 261.63 Hz) | 4 |
 * | SPEC-INT-1..5 | `IntervalTests` | 5 |
 * | SPEC-INT-6..7 | `IntervalPropertyTests` (inversion, round-trip transposition) | 5 |
 * | SPEC-ERR-1..7 | `InvalidStateTests` per type | 3-5 |
 * | SPEC-EMB-1..3 | `static_assert` layout tests, AVR build | 3-5 |
 * | SPEC-SCL-1..4, EMB-5 | `ScalePatternTests`, `ScaleTests`, `ScaleCatalogTests`, manual AVR section inspection | 6 |
 * | SPEC-CHD-1..5, EMB-5 | `ChordPatternTests`, `ChordTests`, `ChordCatalogTests`, `FromScaleTests`, manual AVR section inspection | 7 |
 * | SPEC-KEY-1..3 | `KeyTests` | 8 |
 * | SPEC-TXT-1..5 | `NotationTests` | 8 |
 * | SPEC-RHY-1..4 | `NoteValueTests` (all bases and dot counts) | 9 |
 * | SPEC-RHY-5..7 | `NoteTests` (constructors, invalid state, spelling and movement) | 9 |
 * | SPEC-RHY-8..10 | `MeterTests` (all numerators and denominators, beat derivation) | 9 |
 */
